c2c Codex app-server delivery should reuse the default Codex app-server

Observed with c2c 0.18.0 (built 2026-10-13, commit a7b35338c7395b99d3de15d172c41723d0d9e06e) and Codex CLI 0.162.1.

Summary

An earlier c2c-managed Codex launch started a separate per-instance Codex app-server over a local WebSocket instead of attaching to the normal managed Codex app-server daemon. A later c2c 0.18.0 launch, with the default daemon already healthy, correctly reused the default daemon over a Unix socket. The remaining bug is that this reuse behavior appears conditional or insufficiently documented, and c2c's registry reports the reused instance as app-server unavailable even while it is connected and running.

The earlier separate-server behavior created multiple Codex app-server/control planes with separate lifecycle and state ownership. It caused a long-running Codex session to use the wrong auth/control path during remote compaction and become unusable until the client and app-server were restarted.

Expected behavior

When the default Codex app-server is already running, c2c should reuse it through its normal Unix control socket:

  /home/xertrov/.codex/app-server-control/app-server-control.sock

c2c should register/deliver the managed session through that existing app-server and should not start a second app-server for the same Codex installation and CODEX_HOME. If reuse is impossible, c2c should clearly document the reason and provide an explicit opt-in for the separate-server behavior.

Actual behavior

Earlier c2c 0.18.0 behavior, observed while the default Codex daemon was also running:

A c2c-managed Codex instance was launched as:

  c2c new codex -- resume 01a02fdd-02aa-70e3-9df7-27934234a38d

The c2c wrapper started its own app-server:

  codex app-server --listen ws://127.0.0.1:34737 --ws-auth capability-token ...

and connected the client with:

  codex --remote ws://127.0.0.1:34737 --remote-auth-token-env C2C_CODEX_REMOTE_TOKEN_... resume 01a02fdd-02aa-70e3-9df7-27934234a38d

At the same time, the normal managed Codex daemon was running separately:

  codex app-server --listen unix:// --managed-daemon

A later test after the affected client was closed used c2c 0.18.0 to run:

  cx resume 01a0ef9b-44e6-70c2-8075-8b79adb1b06b

This time c2c did not start a second app-server. The observed process tree was:

  c2c new codex -- resume 01a0ef9b-44e6-70c2-8075-8b79adb1b06b
    node .../codex.js --remote unix:///tmp/codex-daemon-1000/<socket> resume ...
      codex --remote unix:///tmp/codex-daemon-1000/<socket> resume ...

The socket belonged to the existing managed daemon PID 2069932, whose control socket was:

  /home/xertrov/.codex/app-server-control/app-server-control.sock

No new WebSocket app-server listener appeared. The managed daemon owned the resumed session's thread lock. This demonstrates that c2c 0.18.0 can reuse the default app-server under at least some startup conditions.

However, c2c still reported the running instance as:

  codex-vector-brine-2f6b codex running unavailable (pid 2098110)

and its c2c app-server ingress ledger had no error but the c2c registry did not expose the underlying Unix-socket attachment. The reuse decision and health reporting need to be made explicit and tested.

Failure symptoms

The affected session repeatedly produced:

  workspace routing discovery unauthorized (401)
  Error running remote compact task: workspace routing discovery unauthorized (401)

The affected session was a long-running AngelScript API implementation session. Model changes between these IDs did not fix it:

  gpt-6-sol
  gpt-6.1-sol
  openai/gpt-6.1-sol

The persisted session events showed the provider ID as openai for the failing turns, even though the local Codex configuration used OpenCodex. The stored direct ChatGPT OAuth refresh token was revoked, so Codex's workspace-routing/remote-compaction control path returned 401.

The LAN OpenCodex inference path was healthy throughout. Direct Responses requests through the configured service succeeded for all relevant models:

  http://cachy.lan:10100/v1
  gpt-6-sol
  gpt-6.1-sol
  openai/gpt-6.1-sol

A controlled local resume using the default managed app-server and explicit OpenCodex routing succeeded after the c2c client was closed and the managed daemon was restarted:

  codex exec resume -c model_provider=opencodex -c model=gpt-6.1-sol \
    -m gpt-6.1-sol 01a0ef9b-44e6-70c2-8075-8b79adb1b06b

The session compacted and returned OK.

Why this matters

The c2c app-server transport is described by c2c 0.18.0 help as the default and only managed path for supported Codex clients, with hook-backed fallback. The new test proves that c2c can reuse the default daemon, but the earlier run proves that it can also start a dedicated WebSocket app-server. The selection criteria are not visible in the CLI behavior or documentation. Running both modes against the same CODEX_HOME, SQLite state, thread locks, model catalogs, and auth configuration is unsafe.

The registry/health surface also labeled the successfully attached instance as `app-server=unavailable`, even though its client was connected to the default daemon over the Unix socket. That makes operational diagnosis harder and can cause an operator to start or restart the wrong server.

Requested fix

1. Detect the active Codex managed app-server and its control socket.
2. Prefer and document reuse of that app-server for c2c delivery when it belongs to the same CODEX_HOME and Codex installation.
3. Avoid starting a second app-server on a random local WebSocket port in that case.
4. If the dedicated-server fallback is required, report the reason and clearly mark the instance as isolated.
5. Ensure the selected app-server and the Codex client use the same config, provider, model catalog, auth environment, and thread store.
6. Fix c2c instance health reporting so a client attached through the default daemon's Unix socket is not shown as `app-server=unavailable`.
7. If a dedicated app-server is required, fail clearly when another daemon owns the same CODEX_HOME/thread store, or provide an explicit isolated CODEX_HOME and state directory.
8. Add an integration test with the default daemon already running, run `c2c start codex`, and assert that c2c attaches through the daemon's Unix socket and does not spawn a second app-server.
9. Add a separate integration test for the dedicated-server fallback and assert that its lifecycle/state is isolated.
10. Add a regression test for a long-running session whose model provider is OpenCodex and whose remote-compaction/collaboration operation must not silently fall back to direct ChatGPT workspace routing.
11. Document the relationship between c2c's "app-server transport" and Codex's default managed app-server.

Environment

  OS: Linux
  c2c: 0.18.0
  Codex CLI: 0.162.1
  Default Codex provider: OpenCodex over LAN
  OpenCodex endpoint: http://cachy.lan:10100/v1
  Codex CODEX_HOME: /home/xertrov/.codex -> /home/xertrov/.codex-w
  Shared SQLite home: /home/xertrov/.codex-shared/sqlite

No credentials are included in this report. The session ID is included because it is needed to reproduce and inspect the lifecycle issue locally.

Decision: Treat this as one serial implementation lane because the transport selection, instance health reporting, integration tests, and documentation all share the Codex managed-session adapter and must be changed and reviewed together. Prefer the existing default daemon when its same-CODEX_HOME control socket is healthy; preserve a clearly labeled isolated fallback only when reuse is unavailable. The bug report's observed Unix-socket reuse means the implementation should first expose and test the existing selection seam before adding a new daemon protocol.